y you'll see what appears to be  another figure laying in the grass  colored in red i'm guessing that's  supposed to be judith and this is lori  who michael is threatening with the  knife the bigger question i have is  who drew this have michael's art skills  not progressed since age six due to his  incarceration in the sanitarium are we  supposed to believe that he made this  drawing in kindergarten and it's just  been filed away at the school somewhere  ever since or maybe it's just another  kid's drawing that he repurposed to send  his own message just don't even think  about it things will be better for you i  promise to be honest i have a lot of  questions about this movie like how did  the media find out about the babysitter  murders before the sheriff of the town  why does loomis put his face so close to  lori when he's asking if she's okay i'm  sorry i love you are you all right  that's so weird and why are there no  holes in michael's mask after laura  shoots him in the face are we supposed  to believe that both of those shots went  perfectly into the eye holes michael is  21 years old in this movie it's even  called out by dr loomis at one point so  why do the credits say michael myers age  23 they actually made the same mistake  in the first movie but if you do the  math he is 21 not sure how that happens  why does michael's mask look so funny  from a low angle was this entire thing  just an elaborate ruse by jimmy to get a  lap dance from lori strode man you can't  live with them you can't live without  them yeah i don't think that's how the  saying goes help her out lloyd women  can't live with them  can't live without them words of wisdom  the other question i've always had about  this movie comes up in the third act  when loomis marion chambers and the  state marshal show up at the hospital  not realizing that lori is injured in  the parking lot i always found it  pointless how she waits until after  they've gone inside to scream for help  but in analyzing this movie the scene  fits in with the bad dream theme that's  been established not being able to find  your voice totally sounds like something  that would happen in a nightmare i can  even remember having dreams like that as  a kid and then waking up to find myself  mouthing the words with the dry throws i  think the line that ties this movie's  themes together the best comes when  loomis explains the rituals of samhain  in order to appease the gods the druid  priests held fire rituals  prisons of war criminals the insane  animals  were  burned alive in bastards it is  interesting that when he brings up  rituals he specifically mentions burning  alive because as we all know that's  michael meyer's fate in this movie at  least for now and that iconic shot of  michael myers burning might be yet  another direct reference to the thing  from another world which was referenced  very heavily in the first halloween  which was again a teaser for what john  carpenter would go on to work on by  observing the way they died the druids  believed they could see omens of the  future  two thousand years later we've come no  further  samhain isn't  evil spirits  it isn't goblins ghosts or witches  it's the unconscious mind  we're all afraid of the dark inside  ourselves we're all afraid of the dark  inside of ourselves the idea that i've  come back to throughout this entire  video the idea that lori knows in her  subconscious that michael myers is her  brother is the terrifying reality of  this halloween night a reality that  makes even evil spirits goblins ghosts  and witches seem tame by comparison the  idea is hammered home by the final scene  bringing the story full circle lori is  right back where she started moving on  to yet another hospital the ambulance  disappears into what looks like a foggy  dreamscape the news station wwar who we  heard the initial reports from about  michael myers is now on the scene  getting coverage of his latest sets of  victims michael lies 